global.ecosystemextent.iucn_ecosystem_ty.template:
@section(Limitations)
The typologies on which the mapping algorithm builds do not describe all relevant input variables for all target classes. To control for this asymmetry, the algorithm iterates over each class following a predefined mapping order. A class is assigned to a pixel if its conditions are met, in which case classes appearing further down the hierarchy are not considered. As a consequence, a pixel that supports two or more classes is permanently assigned to whichever class is evaluated and confirmed first in the mapping sequence. This may occasionally simplify pixels with natural class transitions. When expected mapping conditions are rejected by all candidate classes, post-processing, gap-filling routines are employed that consider the similarity between class definitions and the available input data.

@section(Data Dependency)
The accuracy of the mapping, and particularly the effectiveness of the fuzzy confidence scoring, is strictly dependent on the spatial resolution, temporal alignment, and completeness of the input environmental rasters, as well as the accuracy of those inputs. Missing data in those inputs, their intrinsic errors, and mismatches with target temporal and spatial mapping resolutions will naturally translate into perceived mapping errors.

